# Example /etc/mitos-sudoers for mitos-utils' `sudo`.
#
# One username per line. A user listed here can run `sudo` after
# entering *their own* password (not root's). Blank lines and lines
# starting with # are ignored.
#
# This is deliberately much simpler than a real /etc/sudoers: no
# per-command rules, no host qualifiers, no NOPASSWD, no groups
# (%wheel-style) -- see sudo(1) and src/common/auth.rs's doc comment
# for why smaller is deliberate here.
#
# This file MUST be owned by root and NOT writable by anyone else
# (e.g. `chown root:root /etc/mitos-sudoers && chmod 644
# /etc/mitos-sudoers`) or `sudo` will refuse to trust it at all --
# a world/group-writable sudoers file would let any user add
# themselves to it.

# alice
# bob
